{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"e7a5bc30-b298-4897-8d1a-7c0f82d9d899","step_index":42,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"grep_search","duration_seconds":0.173005,"tool_info":{"name":"grep_search","parameters":{"Query":"additional_dirs","SearchPath":"/workspace/.worktrees/pr4/docs"},"output":"tools.md:| `permissions` | `{ allow?, deny?, sandboxed?, additional_dirs? }` | profile ceiling | Narrowing only, within the three-owner model (code / project ceiling / this field) — see [`permissions.md`](./permissions.md). `allow` is intersected with the ceiling, `deny` is unioned, `sandboxed` (`boolean`, narrowing only) forces `agy`'s OS sandbox on for this job — on agy 1.1.24 that means no in-workspace shell writes, so builds and `git commit` fail — and `additional_dirs` (extra `--add-dir` roots) is intersected with the project ceiling's own list. |\ntools.md:| `policy_summary` | `{ profile, allow_count, bypass_sandbox, sandbox_forced_by, additional_dirs }` — what the policy actually resolved to. `bypass_sandbox` is what every allowed `run_command` will get as `BypassSandbox`; `sandbox_forced_by` says why it is `false` when it is (`\"profile\"`, `\"ceiling\"`, `\"request\"`) and is `null` otherwise. |\ntools.md:| `blockers` | One `source: \"policy_ceiling\"` entry per `permissions.allow` / `additional_dirs` rule the ceiling refused, each with `actionable` and `remedy`. |\ntools.md:`ceiling` — `{ path, present, extra_allow, extra_deny, sandboxed, additional_dirs }`,\ntools.md:| `policy_ceiling` | A `permissions.allow` / `additional_dirs` entry the project ceiling refused, or the allow-collapse trap. Pre-flight, on `agy_start` only. | yes | n/a — there is no job yet |\ntools.md:| `sandbox` | A known OS-sandbox signature in a `run_command`'s output, on a job that ran sandboxed. A job with `bypass_sandbox: true` gets a warning instead, never this blocker — there was no sandbox to blame. `actionable` is true when `permissions.sandboxed` or `research_readonly` forced the sandbox (retry without / on `general_worker`), false when the ceiling's `sandboxed: true` did (a human flips it); `remedy` names the lever, plus `additional_dirs` for a blocked toolchain path. | depends | yes |\npermissions.md:  \"additional_dirs\": [\"~/.jdks\", \"~/.gradle\"]\npermissions.md:- `additional_dirs` — extra `--add-dir` roots. Measured (M4): the registered path is opened inside the OS sandbox for **reading and executing**, not just for our own containment — the primary lever for a toolchain outside the workspace, e.g. letting `./gradlew` see `~/.jdks`.\npermissions.md:| `additional_dirs` | Each entry must match a ceiling glob, or it is dropped. |\npermissions.md:The reply carries `policy_summary` (`{ profile, allow_count, bypass_sandbox, sandbox_forced_by, additional_dirs }` — `sandbox_forced_by` is `null`, `\"profile\"`, `\"ceiling\"`, or `\"request\"`) and `blockers[]` with `source: \"policy_ceiling\"` per rejected entry. Use `dry_run: true` to see this before spending a turn.\npermissions.md:- `hooks.json` files inside an `additional_dirs` root — untested whether a later group there could override our decision. Low risk: the job cannot write into that root regardless.\npermissions.md:  - In every case, a blocked read/exec of a toolchain outside the workspace is `additional_dirs` in the ceiling, not the sandbox switch."}}}
